{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"46d6c727d3c659a7","name":"card_event_out_with_the_old","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Soviet/WorldAtWar/events/card_event_out_with_the_old","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"OUT WITH THE OLD","text":"Trigger the destruction effects on a damaged Soviet air unit, then Convert it into YAK 9.","flavorText":"At the start of WWII, the Soviet Air Forces fielded outdated aircraft like the I-15 biplane and I-16 monoplane, many of which were outclassed by German fighters. However, the war triggered a rapid transformation in Soviet aircraft design and production. Engineers streamlined manufacturing, adopted mixed-construction techniques, and focused on rugged, combat-effective designs that could be built quickly and in massive numbers. The Yakovlev Yak-9 exemplified this shift. Introduced in 1942, it used lightweight materials and modular construction, allowing rapid adaptation to various combat roles. The Yak-9T carried a 37 mm cannon for tank busting, while other variants served as long-range escorts or dogfighters. Its agility and ease of maintenance made it a favorite among Soviet pilots. Alongside the Yak-9, newer Lavochkin and Ilyushin models replaced older types across the front. Factories evacuated east of the Urals kept up relentless output, delivering modern aircraft even under extreme wartime conditions. By 1945, the Soviet Union’s air force had transformed into a formidable, modern force capable of meeting the Luftwaffe head-on.","faction":"Soviet","type":"order","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":1,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_out_with_the_old","title":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_out_with_the_old_title","SourceString":"OUT WITH THE OLD","LocalizedString":"OUT WITH THE OLD"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Soviet","kredits":1,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_out_with_the_old_text","SourceString":"Trigger the destruction effects on a damaged Soviet air unit, then Convert it into YAK 9.","LocalizedString":"Trigger the destruction effects on a damaged Soviet air unit, then Convert it into YAK 9."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_out_with_the_old_history","SourceString":"At the start of WWII, the Soviet Air Forces fielded outdated aircraft like the I-15 biplane and I-16 monoplane, many of which were outclassed by German fighters. However, the war triggered a rapid transformation in Soviet aircraft design and production. Engineers streamlined manufacturing, adopted mixed-construction techniques, and focused on rugged, combat-effective designs that could be built quickly and in massive numbers. The Yakovlev Yak-9 exemplified this shift. Introduced in 1942, it used lightweight materials and modular construction, allowing rapid adaptation to various combat roles. The Yak-9T carried a 37 mm cannon for tank busting, while other variants served as long-range escorts or dogfighters. Its agility and ease of maintenance made it a favorite among Soviet pilots. Alongside the Yak-9, newer Lavochkin and Ilyushin models replaced older types across the front. Factories evacuated east of the Urals kept up relentless output, delivering modern aircraft even under extreme wartime conditions. By 1945, the Soviet Union’s air force had transformed into a formidable, modern force capable of meeting the Luftwaffe head-on.","LocalizedString":"At the start of WWII, the Soviet Air Forces fielded outdated aircraft like the I-15 biplane and I-16 monoplane, many of which were outclassed by German fighters. However, the war triggered a rapid transformation in Soviet aircraft design and production. Engineers streamlined manufacturing, adopted mixed-construction techniques, and focused on rugged, combat-effective designs that could be built quickly and in massive numbers. The Yakovlev Yak-9 exemplified this shift. Introduced in 1942, it used lightweight materials and modular construction, allowing rapid adaptation to various combat roles. The Yak-9T carried a 37 mm cannon for tank busting, while other variants served as long-range escorts or dogfighters. Its agility and ease of maintenance made it a favorite among Soviet pilots. Alongside the Yak-9, newer Lavochkin and Ilyushin models replaced older types across the front. Factories evacuated east of the Urals kept up relentless output, delivering modern aircraft even under extreme wartime conditions. By 1945, the Soviet Union’s air force had transformed into a formidable, modern force capable of meeting the Luftwaffe head-on."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":true,"cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::NavalWarfare","extraHelpTextBubbleTypes":["EHelpBubbleTypes::Destruction","EHelpBubbleTypes::Convert"],"spawnCardName":"card_unit_yak_9","soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'TACTICAL_AIRSTRIKE_17'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Misc/OrdersFeb17/TACTICAL_AIRSTRIKE_17.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Soviet/World_at_War/T_out_with_the_old.T_out_with_the_old","SubPathString":""},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/OUT_WITH_THE_OLD.OUT_WITH_THE_OLD","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":340.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_out_with_the_old_photo","SourceString":"Parked Yak-9s after the Soviet invasion of Manchuria, Port Arthur, China, September 1945.","LocalizedString":"Parked Yak-9s after the Soviet invasion of Manchuria, Port Arthur, China, September 1945."},"isAttackBuff":true,"isDefenseBuff":true},"variables":[],"functions":[{"name":"CanPlayFromHand","owner":"card_event_out_with_the_old_C","params":[{"name":"canIt","type":"bool","direction":"out"},{"name":"reason","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"reasonParam1","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"reasonParam2","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":